findings.description:
The design assumes flat `.claude/skills/commit.md` files. The repo's actual layout is a directory per skill: `.claude/skills/analyze/SKILL.md`, with frontmatter `name`, `description`, `disable-model-invocation`. The design does not say:
- how a skill directory (possibly with supporting files) becomes one `*.prompt.md`;
- how the output filename is derived;
- how frontmatter maps to the prompt fields the plan lists at line 191 (`name`, `description`, `argument-hint`, `agent`, `model`, `tools`), or what happens to `disable-model-invocation`.

The `CLAUDE.md` check, prompt and `.bak` backup run for every target today, which was harmless with one target. The design (line 110) only says "add copilot safety check (parallel to claude's block)". As written, `cf setup-ide copilot` in a project with an unmanaged `CLAUDE.md` will:
- warn "CLAUDE.md already exists and will be overwritten";
- abort the whole Copilot setup if the user answers N;
- otherwise write `CLAUDE.md.bak`.

That contradicts lines 17 and 55 ("never to `.claude/` or `CLAUDE.md`", "additive only"). The design must state that the Claude block becomes `target === 'claude'` only, and add a test for it.

Step 4 checks only `.github/copilot-instructions.md` and `AGENTS.md`. Hand-written `.github/instructions/*.instructions.md` and `.github/prompts/*.prompt.md` that collide by name are overwritten with no prompt and no backup. Existing Copilot users are the people most likely to have such files. Plan line 197 asks for the marker convention "for Copilot target files" and line 214 for a prompt on "existing unmanaged files".

Also unspecified:
- one prompt or two when both top-level files are unmanaged;
- the backup filenames;
- the `.bak`-already-exists case (the Claude path silently skips the backup at `setup-ide.ts:83-88`, so the second overwrite is unrecoverable);
- the mixed case where one file is managed and the other is not.

The script carries most of the slice: compilation, three translations and marker emission. Its entire specification is line 114. Missing items:
- how `alwaysApply` is detected;
- output naming;
- where the marker sits relative to the YAML frontmatter that must open `.instructions.md` and `.prompt.md` files (line 65 says "first 20 lines");
- whether `description` and `name` are carried over.

Every item in Test Coverage (lines 123-129) is CLI-side with `execFileSync` mocked. Plan success criteria 211 (files generated) and 216 (path→applyTo correct) therefore have no test anywhere.

The script claims could not be verified because `project-documents/ai-project-guide/` is empty in this worktree. The only in-tree copy, `project-documents/scripts/setup-ide`, emits no marker and validates `globs`, not `paths` (lines 97-118, 181-184). It is evidently stale, since the root `CLAUDE.md:3` has the marker.

Line 83 says absent `paths` defaults to `applyTo: "**"`, and then says such files are not emitted as instruction files. Either the default is dead, or "absent paths" and "alwaysApply" are separate signals. The design does not say which, or where a rule with neither goes.

Plan line 200 requires the translator to "handle both forms"; only the block-list form is shown. Also unaddressed:
- inline arrays (`paths: ["a","b"]`);
- the trailing space after `paths:` in real files (`.claude/rules/typescript.md:3`);
- globs containing commas (`**/*.{ts,tsx}`), which a comma-joined `applyTo` would split.

These matter because the translator is bash/awk, not a YAML parser.
